6
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:08,300
 - We got our start in the early days of the web

7
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,300
 as a group of disaffected webmasters

8
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,580
 who were using a piece of freely available web software,

9
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,880
 but had difficulty with it.

10
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:16,940
 We were fixing bugs.

11
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:18,740
 We were sharing these bug fixes with each other,

12
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:21,560
 like baseball trading cards, if you will,

13
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:23,540
 these patches, as it's called.

14
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,780
 And one day we discovered the group

15
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,340
 that put out the web server that we were using

16
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:30,300
 basically folded when all their developers left

17
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,540
 to go join a brand new company called Netscape.

18
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,540
 So a bunch of us decided that,

19
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:36,360
 hey, we're dependent upon this software.

20
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:39,140
 We don't want to become full-time web server developers,

21
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:42,120
 but we want to be able to use this thing

22
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,980
 that we've had for free and be able to improve it

23
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:45,580
 and all that kind of stuff.

24
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:47,040
 We looked at the license of the code,

25
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,580
 and the license said, here's the software.

26
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,020
 Do whatever you want with it.

27
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:53,260
 Don't blame us when it breaks, right?

28
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,220
 And we said, hey, that's a pretty good bargain.

29
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:56,660
 Why don't we pass the same bargain

30
00:02:56,660 --> 00:02:58,940
 onto the next group of people, right?

31
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:01,820
 So we formed a mailing list, right?

32
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,140
 And this was mostly, again, webmasters

33
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:07,700
 and people working at some early internet service providers

34
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:11,700
 or website design companies or places like Amazon

35
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,980
 or the internet movie database.

36
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:16,620
 And we combined our patches together

37
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:20,140
 and decided to call it a patchy server for that reason

38
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:21,820
 and went forward.

39
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:23,740
 And really the model of how we worked

40
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:27,780
 was based upon kind of us as a group, as peers,

41
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:32,100
 proposing ideas, vetting each other's ideas and patches

42
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,420
 and fixing bugs as a group, as a team.

43
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:37,700
 None of us had met in person, well, some of us had met,

44
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:41,300
 but as a group, we didn't meet in person until 1998,

45
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:43,500
 really three years after we got our start.

46
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:44,860
 And long after, by the way,

47
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:47,900
 we'd become the most predominant web server product

48
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:49,260
 on the planet.

49
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:51,620
 And yet at this time, still, no money, no dime,

50
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:54,460
 not direct to us from this piece of open source software,

51
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:57,300
 but plenty of us made our living

52
00:03:57,300 --> 00:03:59,900
 off of building things on top of this piece.

53
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:01,300
 And that's really the story, I think,

54
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:04,500
 of kind of successful open source projects writ large,

55
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:08,060
 which is people working together on common technologies

56
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:09,580
 to solve common problems

57
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:11,620
 so they can go off and make money on other places

58
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:13,980
 or so they can have fun, they can try new ideas,

59
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:17,300
 they can be experimental, right?

60
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:21,180
 And that's really the same story of Apache and of Linux

61
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,180
 and all these other open source projects.

62
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:26,740
 - So one of the core things about Apache

63
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:28,740
 is the concept of meritocracy.

64
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:32,660
 Was that a difficult thing to make happen?

65
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:35,940
 I mean, I look at your bylaws and I say,

66
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:38,740
 somebody crafted those bylaws carefully,

67
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:40,820
 or was this just kind of like dumb luck?

68
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:42,340
 Is this just kind of how it worked out?

69
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:45,100
 Were there any like fights over the bylaws,

70
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:47,540
 or did the bylaws just kind of come naturally?

71
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:49,820
 - So let's say you've got a group of your neighbors,

72
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:52,820
 you know, say a four or five block radius, right?

73
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:55,500
 Group of your neighbors, you're on a mailing list, right?

74
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:57,500
 And you kind of wanna be social with each other

75
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:59,180
 because you have to see each other every day.

76
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:02,220
 You know, you don't wanna pick fights necessarily.

77
00:05:02,220 --> 00:05:04,180
 But you wanna get something done and you decide that,

78
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,780
 hey, let's throw a picnic on some Sunday, right?

79
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:09,300
 So as a group of neighbors, how are you going to decide

80
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:12,060
 how to throw a big group picnic, right?

81
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:14,060
 Well, somebody's gonna say, hey, I have a big grill.

82
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:15,260
 You know, somebody else will say,

83
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:18,620
 I have this great meat place that I go and buy my meat from.

84
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:19,500
 I'll get it there.

85
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:21,980
 Somebody else says, I make a wicked potato salad

86
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:23,100
 or something like that.

87
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:26,020
 And, you know, by people showing up and volunteering things

88
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:27,180
 and saying what they can do

89
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:29,700
 and building upon each other's ideas,

90
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:32,380
 you generally find a way to do it.

91
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:36,740
 That carries a tone of respect for other people's ideas

92
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:40,700
 that, you know, recognizes that passion and motivation

93
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,420
 are some of the most finite resources in the world, right?

94
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:45,420
 And you have to cherish that.

95
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:46,460
 You don't wanna, you know,

96
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:49,780
 naysay somebody's idea just out of hand, right?

97
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:52,540
 And so I think it just kind of fell out naturally

98
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:54,700
 from, you know, kind of the golden rule

99
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:56,860
 of do unto others and that kind of thing.

100
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,420
 Didn't seem like magic to us, I guess,

101
00:05:58,420 --> 00:05:59,900
 when we were doing it.

102
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:02,660
 But yeah, it turns out to be not that hard

103
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:03,980
 to be able to work together

104
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:05,740
 when people have the same common goal,

105
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:07,660
 which is let's build a product

106
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:09,560
 that does all this great stuff.

107
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:13,140
 One thing that we did do that made it easy

108
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:14,260
 to make some of these decisions

109
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:17,460
 was to have a very modular API,

110
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:19,540
 which made it easy for us to be able to say,

111
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:21,940
 "Hey, if you want that special cool feature,

112
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:25,180
 "do it as a separate thing and make it successful,

113
00:06:25,180 --> 00:06:27,500
 "and we'll decide whether to bring this into the product

114
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:30,260
 "once it's become successful or not," right?

115
00:06:30,260 --> 00:06:32,660
 Another key thing that plays into this

116
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:34,860
 that is true of all open source projects

117
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:38,500
 is that an open source license, like we had on ours,

118
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:40,300
 that Linux has, et cetera,

119
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:42,780
 carries with it something called the right to fork,

120
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:45,980
 which means that if I were to go all Colonel Kurtz

121
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:49,380
 on the project and start saying we're gonna go here

122
00:06:49,380 --> 00:06:50,700
 and no one else wanted to follow,

123
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,340
 well, all of those other people

124
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:54,420
 could decide to pick up the code

125
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:56,420
 and go start a different project somewhere else

126
00:06:56,420 --> 00:06:57,540
 if they couldn't kick me out,

127
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:00,620
 which is probably what they would have tried to do first.

128
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:03,260
 This right to fork means that

129
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:05,540
 you don't have to have any tolerance for dictators.

130
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:07,260
 You don't have to deal with people

131
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:09,080
 who make bad technical decisions.

132
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:12,540
 You can take that future into your hands,

133
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:15,140
 and if you find a group of other people who agree with you,

134
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:17,820
 you can go on and create a new project around it.

135
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:20,340
 So I think that rule, that right to fork,

136
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:22,860
 limits the kind of excesses that we see

137
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:24,260
 whenever we start to talk about

138
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:27,720
 how do groups make decisions and conflict arises,

139
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:29,220
 how do you deal with that conflict?

140
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:32,260
 And it means that your style of leadership

141
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:33,900
 isn't so much one of control

142
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:36,060
 and plotting moves ahead of time,

143
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:40,100
 but instead one of being able to get people on your side,

144
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:43,120
 convince them that you're gonna value their efforts,

145
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:45,740
 value the contributions that they make.

146
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:47,500
 - Tell me a little bit about what you're doing now

147
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,380
 and how sort of the Apache work

148
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:52,440
 has flowed into what your current work is.

149
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:54,220
 - Well, I think what we realized

150
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:56,280
 kind of early on with Apache is that

151
00:07:56,280 --> 00:07:58,500
 even though we were really carrying forward

152
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:01,040
 the traditions from the Internet Engineering Task Force,

153
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:03,860
 from the BSD community and the Linux communities

154
00:08:03,860 --> 00:08:05,600
 in terms of how to write software,

155
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:07,500
 and even though we were using tools

156
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,740
 available to the open source world like CVS

157
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:12,500
 and web-based issue trackers and such,

158
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:16,380
 I came to realize that there was a certain kind of,

159
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:18,260
 that if you could define how it was

160
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:20,020
 at Apache Build Software,

161
00:08:20,020 --> 00:08:21,940
 define it from the perspective of process

162
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:23,620
 and the perspective of tools,

163
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:25,840
 that was something that if you could

164
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:28,000
 distill it down to a science and make it repeatable,

165
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,260
 you could take it to the rest of the software industry.

166
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:32,620
 And you could do that for more open source projects,

167
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:33,660
 brand new ones,

168
00:08:33,660 --> 00:08:36,180
 or you could even do it inside of companies.

169
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:40,740
 We, on the outside of big companies like an IBM or an HP,

170
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:42,500
 we tend to think that all those companies

171
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:44,660
 must have that innovation problem licked, right?

172
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:46,540
 They must know how to write software,

173
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:50,560
 great software that maximizes the intellectual capital

174
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:52,280
 that they're paying for every day, right?

175
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:54,580
 Turns out a lot of companies are actually

176
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:57,020
 really bad at getting their engineers,

177
00:08:57,020 --> 00:09:00,580
 getting their employees to produce the smartest things,

178
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:03,740
 to reuse each other's work as well as they could,

179
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:06,000
 and as well as the open source community does.

180
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,620
 And so I started a company in 1999

181
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:11,020
 on the premise of let's figure this out,

182
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:13,540
 let's put this in a bottle if we can or define it,

183
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:15,660
 and let's take it to the rest of the industry

184
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,700
 in the form of a collaboration platform

185
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:23,020
 targeted at version control, issue tracking,

186
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:26,380
 and also capturing the ad hoc, you know,

187
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:28,780
 free form conversation that takes place

188
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:30,220
 in open source projects.

189
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:31,860
 And so part of it is that,

190
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:35,180
 part of it as well is educating communities

191
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:38,060
 on best practices around open source engineering.

192
00:09:38,060 --> 00:09:40,380
 How do you have a development process

193
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:42,500
 that is transparent and accessible

194
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:44,620
 to everyone in an organization

195
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:46,100
 rather than something that is, you know,

196
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:48,060
 three people in a cubicle making a decision

197
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:49,880
 about, you know, the right kind of decision,

198
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:54,880
 or a way to implement a particular technical solution, right?

199
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:57,780
 - So finish this sentence, CollabNet is?

200
00:09:57,780 --> 00:09:59,740
 - CollabNet is a products and services company

201
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:03,260
 focused on replicating open source collaboration

202
00:10:03,260 --> 00:10:06,100
 practices and tools to the rest of the software industry.

203
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:08,820
 - Perfect.

204
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:10,380
 Now we're on.

205
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:11,780
 - Hi there.

206
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:14,420
 I'm here at North Carolina State University

207
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:15,820
 at a meeting hosted by IBM

208
00:10:15,820 --> 00:10:19,800
 talking about potential for open source in K-12.

209
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:22,240
 I was very, very fortunate to be on a wonderful panel

210
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:24,660
 with Brian Bellendorf from Apache Foundation.

211
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:28,020
 Brian spoke with the Sakai community last June

212
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:29,500
 in Baltimore as our keynote,

213
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:31,820
 and I was able to talk to Brian

214
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:34,620
 ask him a few things about the beginning of Apache and what he's up to now.

